  {
    "pkattachmentId": 40313,
    "bookid": 45,
    "chapterId": 196,
    "fileId": 22788,
    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p>Diagnostic criteria for neurofibromatosis type&nbsp;1 (NF1)</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "Section contributors - separate source citation not necessary",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoNormalTable\" style=\"width: 16.0cm; background: #DAEEF3; border-collapse: collapse;\" border=\"0\" width=\"605\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 10.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 16.0cm; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 10.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"605\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"page-break-after: avoid;\"><strong><em>Essential:</em></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 10.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 16.0cm; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 10.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"605\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black;\">A clinical diagnosis of NF1 requires the presence of at least two of the following features:</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Six or more caf&eacute;-au-lait macules (diameter &gt;&nbsp;5&nbsp;mm in children, &gt;&nbsp;15&nbsp;mm in adults)</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Two or more cutaneous or subcutaneous neurofibromas or one plexiform neurofibroma</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Axillary/inguinal freckling</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Optic pathway glioma&nbsp;/ pilocytic astrocytoma</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Two or more iris hamartomas (Lisch nodules)</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">Distinctive bony abnormality (tibial dysplasia, pseudarthrosis, orbital dysplasia)</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; text-indent: -18.0pt;\"><span style=\"font-family: Symbol;\">&middot;<span style=\"font: 7.0pt 'Times New Roman';\">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; </span></span><span style=\"color: black;\">First-degree relative with NF1 (by the above criteria)</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 10.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 16.0cm; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 10.2pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"605\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black;\">In the future, these criteria may be revised to include genetic testing and other common newly recognized clinical features. In young children, multiple caf&eacute;-au-lait macules are usually the only clinical sign of NF1, such that in the absence of an affected parent, a diagnosis of NF1 cannot be rendered. Multiple caf&eacute;-au-lait spots with or without skinfold freckling can be seen in other conditions, including Legius syndrome (MIM number: 611431). Multiple neurofibromas can also be associated with certain forms of Noonan syndrome, making molecular analysis of the <em>NF1</em> gene increasingly more important for diagnostic purposes </span><span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{19920235; 25074460; 26337637}</span><span style=\"color: black;\">. In children with multiple caf&eacute;-au-lait spots and malignant tumours (e.g. leukaemia), constitutive mismatch repair deficiency should also be considered </span><span class=\"Ref-PMID\">{30415209}</span><span style=\"color: black;\">.</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>",
